Build a new generic job for managing Kubernetes Deployments across the cluster. Use the existing application architecture, job framework, RBAC model, Pod Metrics discovery/status logic, UI conventions, and job-start YAML namespace configuration. Do not break or refactor existing jobs unnecessarily.

Model this around Deployments, not Pod Lifecycle.

CORE FUNCTIONALITY

1. Discovery
- Discover namespaces dynamically from the existing job-start YAML/application configuration.
- Apply the new namespace-scoped RBAC layer to determine which namespaces the current user can operate on.
- Users without lifecycle privileges must still be able to discover deployments, inspect deployment details, pods, containers, status, events, etc.
- Discover deployments dynamically; never hardcode deployment names or pod names.
- Show each Deployment with:
  - status/health
  - desired/ready/available replicas
  - individual pods
  - containers/sidecars
  - restart counts
  - rollout state
  - relevant age/details
- Reuse existing Kubernetes discovery/status functionality where appropriate, but keep this job independently usable.

2. Deployment actions
- Restart Deployment using a proper Kubernetes Deployment rolling-restart mechanism, not scale-to-zero-and-back.
- Stop Deployment:
  - capture the current desired replica count before scaling to 0
  - persist that previous replica count with the operation/state so it can be restored reliably
- Start Deployment:
  - restore the previously captured replica count
- Scale Deployment explicitly when required.
- For this job, Start/Stop/Restart should be controlled by the same namespace-level lifecycle permission. Do not create separate user permissions for the three actions.
- Operations must be asynchronous and show live progress until completion/failure.
- Support selecting/restarting multiple pods where appropriate, but keep Deployment restart as the primary operation and ensure the behavior is safe and deterministic.

3. Live operation state
Create a polished operation workflow that clearly shows:
- request accepted
- operation started
- rollout/progress state
- pods terminating/recreating/updating
- pods becoming Running
- readiness progression
- final completion
- failure/timeout

Do not dump raw Kubernetes startup logs into the UI.

Translate Kubernetes state into concise human-readable diagnostics such as:
- CrashLoopBackOff
- ImagePullBackOff
- readiness/liveness/startup probe failures
- OOMKilled
- scheduling failures
- image pull failures
- container initialization failures
- timeout/not-ready states

Use Kubernetes pod status, container status and Events as the primary diagnostic sources. Show recent logs only as an optional diagnostic view when useful.

4. RBAC
Extend the existing application/job RBAC architecture with a reusable namespace-scoped permission model.

Add a new job-based RBAC entry/group for this generic Deployment management job.

The model should effectively be:

existing application/user RBAC
    +
job permission
    +
namespace scope

Example:
- Developer A → Deployment Management → dev namespace
- QA users → Deployment Management → qa namespace
- DevOps/Admin → Deployment Management → permitted/all namespaces

Namespace access must NOT be hardcoded in frontend code.

Use the existing job-start YAML/application namespace configuration so newly added applications/namespaces automatically become discoverable where applicable.

Important:
- Unauthorized users can still Discover/Inspect.
- Lifecycle action controls must NOT be rendered for users without lifecycle permission for that namespace.
- Backend must independently enforce the same authorization. Never rely on hiding UI buttons as security.
- Existing RBAC behavior for all other jobs must remain unchanged.
- Design this namespace-scoped permission layer so future jobs can reuse it.

5. Audit / operational logs
Every lifecycle operation must record:
- user
- timestamp
- namespace
- deployment
- operation
- previous replica count where applicable
- resulting state
- success/failure
- failure reason
- operation/job ID

Audit information must be accessible only to authorized Admin/DevOps users.

Do not expose the audit history to normal developers/testers merely because they can operate deployments.

Reuse the existing job history/logging infrastructure where appropriate rather than creating an unnecessary parallel logging system.

UI / FRONTEND REQUIREMENT

This is the highest priority.

The UI must be substantially more modern and polished than the existing application screens. Treat this as a showcase-quality frontend implementation.

Before coding, inspect the existing UI, screenshots/components, Pod Metrics screen, theme system, component library, frontend framework, and existing animation patterns. Preserve compatibility with the current application, but do not constrain the new screen to the old visual design.

Push the UI substantially further:
- premium modern dark operations-console aesthetic
- rich but purposeful animations
- smooth page/row expansion and collapse
- animated status transitions
- deployment health indicators
- animated replica/pod state changes
- polished progress timeline for lifecycle operations
- modern cards and data visualization
- excellent spacing and typography
- subtle hover/focus states
- responsive layout
- skeleton/loading states
- empty states
- error states
- confirmation flows
- contextual action menus
- real-time progress updates
- deployment → pods → containers hierarchy
- clear visual distinction between healthy, updating, degraded and failed states
- avoid visual clutter despite exposing rich information
- do not expose raw Kubernetes complexity unless the user asks to inspect it

The main Deployment screen should feel like a modern Kubernetes operations product rather than a conventional CRUD/table page.

Suggested experience:

Namespace selector
→ Deployment overview/health summary
→ Deployment list
→ expandable Deployment rows
→ individual pods
→ deployment detail drawer/page
→ lifecycle action
→ animated operation timeline
→ live completion/failure state

The lifecycle progress view should visually communicate something like:

Initiated → Updating → Pods Replacing → Waiting for Ready → Completing → Finished

with live pod-level progress.

For failures, provide an intelligent diagnostic summary with an option to inspect Events and recent logs rather than flooding the main screen with logs.

Use the best modern frontend libraries already compatible with the project. If introducing a new library materially improves animation/data visualization/UI quality, evaluate it first and use a well-maintained compatible library rather than unnecessarily adding dependencies.

Do not introduce a completely separate frontend stack just for this job.

JOB NAME

Choose a generic, professional name suitable for a reusable Kubernetes Deployment management capability. Avoid names tied specifically to restart or a particular application. Use the chosen name consistently across backend, frontend, RBAC and job history.

IMPLEMENTATION APPROACH

First inspect the repository and understand:
- existing job architecture
- job-start YAML structure
- existing RBAC/user/group implementation
- Pod Metrics discovery/status implementation
- existing async job execution
- WebSocket/live update mechanisms
- existing audit/job history
- frontend component/theme architecture

Then implement the feature incrementally without breaking existing functionality.

Add/update tests for:
- namespace discovery
- namespace-scoped authorization
- authorized vs unauthorized lifecycle actions
- Deployment discovery
- restart
- stop with replica preservation
- start restoring the saved replica count
- asynchronous lifecycle state transitions
- failure/timeout handling
- audit records
- existing RBAC regression coverage
- frontend behavior for users with and without lifecycle privileges

Do not hardcode sample namespaces, deployments or pods. Use realistic mock/test data only inside tests.

Run the existing test suite plus relevant frontend checks/build/lint/type checks before considering the implementation complete.

Most importantly: do not settle for a functional but ordinary UI. The Deployment Management screen is specifically intended to showcase high-end frontend engineering and should be treated as a flagship screen in the application.
